lesson art-blue-prints v=1 format=teach grade=2 unit=4 subject=art levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Blue prints from the sun"
  goal "Explain how a sun print is made, match a print to the plant that made it, and make a print of your own with shapes laid on paper."

  look {
    scene 25x11 {
      sunprint pic pairs=[3, 5, 4] made=1 show="match" at=canvas(1, 1)
    }
    say """
      Anna Atkins made a book of seaweed pictures in 1843 without drawing them, and later printed
      ferns the same way. She laid each plant on paper that turns blue in sunlight and left it in
      the sun. Where the plant lay, the sun could not reach, so its shape stayed white.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice art.which-fern-printed count=2 seed=3
    practice art.sun-or-shade count=2 seed=1
    level easy {
      worked art.hole-or-shape s="leaf" w="card" level=medium
    }
    practice art.hole-or-shape count=2 seed=2
    practice art.leaflets-both-sides count=2 hard-count=1 seed=1
    level hard {
      practice art.ferns-crossed count=1 seed=1
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show art.ferns-crossed k=0 easy-k=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show art.make-a-sun-print
  }
  remember {
    say "Where the sun reaches, the paper turns blue. Where a plant lies, it stays white."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Anna Atkins (1799 to 1871) was a botanist, and her Photographs of British Algae, published in
    parts from October 1843, is considered the first book illustrated with photographic images. The
    paper is a cyanotype: it is coated with iron salts that turn Prussian blue in sunlight and wash
    out white where nothing reached them. Sun print paper is sold for children and works on a sunny
    windowsill in a few minutes.

    The questions are marked by the machine. The first matches a print to its fern by counting one
    side of each. The others ask the child to reason about where light went: which part the sun
    reached, what a card with a hole does compared with the shape cut out of it, and how the fern's
    two matching sides make counting its leaflets quicker. The two-star question asks what happens
    where two ferns cross, and the answer, white, surprises children who think in paint, where two
    layers make a colour darker.

    On screen the print is made with the stencils: lay shapes on the sheet, brush blue over
    everything, and lift them off. Respond by pointing at the white shapes and asking the child where
    each one lay. Ask where they thought the white would be before they lifted the shapes.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the ferns to tell apart have few leaflets, the question asks where the sun reached, a hole in card is worked once in the teacher's pen before shapes laid on the paper, the ferns have three or four leaflets a side, the two-star asks about the paper the ferns did not cover, and the three-star is as written."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the ferns to tell apart have many leaflets, the question asks where the fern lay, the cards have holes, the ferns have six or seven leaflets a side and the second count makes way for a second pair of crossed ferns, the two-star asks about the crossing, which stays white, and the three-star is as written."
  }
}
